.SS Abstract
\f[CR]INLINE\f[R] and \f[CR]FORCE_INLINE\f[R] are function modifiers
used to request that a compiler treat a function as an inline function.
.PP
\f[CR]INLINE\f[R] provides a portable way to request normal inlining.
The compiler may choose whether or not to actually substitute the
function\(aqs code at each call site.
.PP
\f[CR]FORCE_INLINE\f[R] expresses a stronger request: when the compiler
supports such a feature, it tells the compiler to make a stronger effort
to inline the function.
It may use compiler\-specific extensions such as
\f[CR]__forceinline\f[R] or \f[CR]__attribute__((always_inline))\f[R].
.PP
Inlining can reduce the overhead of a function call, but it may also
increase the size of the generated program.
\f[B]Neither \f[CB]INLINE\f[B] nor \f[CB]FORCE_INLINE\f[B] should be
interpreted as a guarantee that the resulting machine code will contain
no function call.\f[R] The exact behaviour depends on the compiler and
its optimisation settings.
.PP
\f[B]Example\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// Inline function:\f[R]
INLINE int test(void)
{
    \f[B]return\f[R] (15 + 42);
}

\f[I]// Used in main:\f[R]
int main(void)
{
    printf(\(dq%d\(dq, test());
    \f[B]return\f[R] (0);
}
.EE
.PP
A compiler may effectively turn the call into something equivalent to:
.IP
.EX
int main(void)
{
    printf(\(dq%d\(dq, (15 + 42));
    \f[B]return\f[R] (0);
}
.EE
.PP
The exact generated code is compiler\-dependent.
.PP
This is only an example.
You are free to put whatever valid code is appropriate inside an inline
function, but some constructs can prevent or complicate inlining.
See \f[B]How Not to Use It\f[R] below.
