.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS INLINE
.IP
.EX
#define INLINE
.EE
.PP
\f[CR]INLINE\f[R] requests normal inline semantics for a function.
.PP
The exact definition of \f[CR]INLINE\f[R] depends on the compiler.
For example, it may expand to the standard C/C++ \f[CR]inline\f[R]
keyword or to a compiler\-specific equivalent such as
\f[CR]__inline\f[R] or \f[CR]__inline__\f[R].
.PP
Just put this tag at the beginning of the function declaration.
.PP
\f[B]Example\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
INLINE void function(void)
{
    . . .
}
.EE
.PP
\f[CR]INLINE\f[R] is a \f[B]request\f[R], not a guarantee.
The compiler is still free to emit an ordinary function call when it
determines that inlining is undesirable or impossible.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS FORCE_INLINE
.IP
.EX
#define FORCE_INLINE
.EE
.PP
\f[CR]FORCE_INLINE\f[R] is intended for functions where inlining is
particularly desirable.
.PP
Where supported, it uses the compiler\(aqs strongest practical inline
mechanism.
For example, MSVC may use \f[CR]__forceinline\f[R], while GCC and Clang
may use \f[CR]__attribute__((always_inline))\f[R].
.PP
\f[B]Example\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
FORCE_INLINE int function(int a, int b)
{
    \f[B]return\f[R] (a + b);
}
.EE
.PP
Unlike \f[CR]INLINE\f[R], \f[CR]FORCE_INLINE\f[R] communicates that the
programmer considers inlining especially important.
.PP
However, \f[B]\f[CB]FORCE_INLINE\f[B] is still not an absolute
guarantee\f[R].
The compiler can reject or ignore an aggressive inline request in
situations where inlining is impossible, incompatible with the current
compilation mode, or otherwise unsupported.
.PP
Use \f[CR]FORCE_INLINE\f[R] sparingly.
Overusing it can increase executable size, increase instruction\-cache
pressure, and sometimes make performance worse rather than better.
.PP
