.PP
In general:
.IP
.EX
INLINE int normal_function(void)
{
    . . .
}
.EE
.PP
should be preferred when the function is simply a good candidate for
inlining.
.PP
Use:
.IP
.EX
FORCE_INLINE int performance_critical_function(void)
{
    . . .
}
.EE
.PP
when you have a specific reason to strongly encourage inlining.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[B]How NOT to Use It\f[R]
There are no universal rules saying that an inline function
\f[B]cannot\f[R] contain particular C or C++ constructs.
Most of the following constructs are legal.
.PP
The important question is whether they make inlining impossible,
undesirable, compiler\-dependent, or less useful.
.SS 1. \f[B]Don\(aqt assume \f[CB]static inline\f[B] prevents inlining.\f[R]
\f[CR]static inline\f[R] does \f[B]not\f[R] cancel the effect of
\f[CR]INLINE\f[R].
.PP
In fact, \f[CR]static inline\f[R] is often useful for functions defined
in headers because it gives the function internal linkage.
.PP
For example:
.IP
.EX
static INLINE int test(void)
{
    \f[B]return\f[R] (15 + 42);
}
.EE
.PP
is perfectly valid on compilers supporting the corresponding inline
semantics.
.PP
However, \f[CR]static inline\f[R] has different linkage semantics from a
non\-static inline function.
Whether it is appropriate depends on how the function is declared and
where the header is used.
.PP
\f[B]Don\(aqt use \f[CB]static\f[B] merely because you think it makes a
function inline faster.\f[R] It does not.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS 2. \f[B]Don\(aqt assume every function can be completely inlined.\f[R]
Some functions are simply poor candidates for inlining.
.PP
For example:
.IP
.EX
INLINE void test(void)
{
    LAYER:

    . . .

    \f[B]goto\f[R] LAYER;
}
.EE
.PP
\f[CR]goto\f[R] itself does \f[B]not\f[R] make an inline function
invalid.
.PP
However, complicated control flow can make optimisation and inlining
more difficult, especially when combined with other constructs.
.PP
The same applies to large functions:
.IP
.EX
FORCE_INLINE void enormous_function(void)
{
    . . .
    \f[I]/* Hundreds or thousands of lines of code... */\f[R]
}
.EE
.PP
Forcing a huge function to be inlined everywhere can make the resulting
program larger and potentially slower.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS 3. \f[B]Static local variables are not inherently forbidden.\f[R]
A static local variable can legally exist inside an inline function in
many C and C++ contexts:
.IP
.EX
INLINE int test(void)
{
    static int dog;

    \f[B]return\f[R] (++dog);
}
.EE
.PP
However, static local variables have \f[B]persistent storage and shared
state\f[R], which may make the function behave very differently from a
simple, side\-effect\-free inline function.
.PP
They can also interact with language\-specific inline and linkage rules.
.PP
Therefore, avoid static local variables in small utility functions
unless their persistent state is intentional.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS 4. \f[B]Recursion cannot generally be completely inlined.\f[R]
An inline function can call itself:
.IP
.EX
INLINE int factorial(int a)
{
    \f[B]if\f[R] (a <= 1)
        \f[B]return\f[R] (1);

    \f[B]return\f[R] (a * factorial(a \- 1));
}
.EE
.PP
This is legal.
.PP
However, a recursive call cannot normally be expanded indefinitely.
Eventually the compiler must leave a function call, transform the
recursion, or otherwise handle it without fully inlining every recursive
invocation.
.PP
Therefore, \f[CR]INLINE\f[R] does \f[B]not\f[R] mean:
.RS
.PP
\(dqEvery call must disappear.\(dq
.RE
.PP
Recursive functions are generally poor candidates for
\f[CR]FORCE_INLINE\f[R].
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS 5. \f[B]Variadic functions may have compiler\-specific limitations.\f[R]
A variadic function such as:
.IP
.EX
INLINE void inline_test(int a, ...)
{
    va_list list;

    . . .
}
.EE
.PP
is not universally forbidden from being inline.
.PP
However, variadic functions can be more complicated for compilers to
optimise, and their exact interaction with inline semantics can depend
on the compiler and language standard.
.PP
In particular, don\(aqt assume that \f[CR]FORCE_INLINE\f[R] can make
every variadic function disappear completely at every call site.
.PP
If a function does not actually need to be variadic, prefer a fixed
parameter list.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS 6. \f[B]Don\(aqt make everything \f[CB]FORCE_INLINE\f[B].\f[R]
This is probably the most important rule.
.PP
Inlining is not automatically an optimisation.
.PP
For example:
.IP
.EX
FORCE_INLINE void enormous_function(void)
{
    . . .
}
.EE
.PP
If this function is called from many different places, forcing its body
into every call site can significantly increase the size of the
generated program.
.PP
Larger code can cause worse instruction\-cache behaviour and can
sometimes make the program \f[B]slower\f[R].
.PP
Use \f[CR]FORCE_INLINE\f[R] when there is a specific performance or
implementation reason for it, not simply because the function is small.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS 7. \f[B]Don\(aqt rely on the source code to determine the final machine code.\f[R]
This:
.IP
.EX
INLINE int test(void)
{
    \f[B]return\f[R] (15 + 42);
}
.EE
.PP
does not guarantee that the compiler will generate a function containing
the addition.
.PP
The compiler may instead constant\-fold the expression:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]return\f[R] (57);
.EE
.PP
or optimise the entire call away.
.PP
Likewise, a compiler may decide that a function marked \f[CR]INLINE\f[R]
should remain an ordinary function call.
.PP
The final machine code depends on the compiler, target architecture,
compilation options, optimisation level, and surrounding code.
